[[Image:Ravel - String Quartet, mov. I motive.png|thumb|Motif from [[Maurice Ravel|Ravel]]'s [[String Quartet (Ravel)|String Quartet]], first movement.{{sfn|White|1976|p=30}} {{audio|Ravel - String Quartet, mov. I motive.mid|Play}}]]

In [[music]], a '''motif''' ({{IPAc-en|audio=en-us-motif.ogg|m|oʊ|ˈ|t|i:|f}}) or '''motive''' is a short musical idea,<ref>{{Cite book |last=Drabkin |first=William |url=https://archive.org/details/newgrovedictiona0017unse/page/227/mode/1up |title=The new Grove dictionary of music and musicians |publisher=Grove |others=Stanley Sadie, John Tyrrell |year=2001 |isbn=1-56159-239-0 |edition=2nd |volume=17 |location=New York |pages=227–228 |oclc=44391762}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Nattiez |first=Jean-Jacques |title=Music and discourse : toward a semiology of music |date=1990 |publisher=Princeton University Press |isbn=0-691-09136-6 |location=Princeton, N.J. |oclc=21524730}}</ref> [[Salience (neuroscience)|salient]] recurring [[Figure (music)|figure]], musical fragment, or succession of notes that has some special importance in or is characteristic of a [[musical composition|composition]]. The motif is the smallest structural unit possessing [[theme (music)|thematic]] identity.{{sfn|White|1976|pp=26–27}}

